GFG — Tree Boundary Traversal
Problem Understanding

In Boundary Traversal of a Binary Tree, we traverse the boundary of the tree in anti-clockwise order:

1. Root
2. Left boundary (excluding leaves)
3. All leaf nodes from left → right
4. Right boundary (excluding leaves), from bottom → top

For example:

                 1
               /   \
              2     7
             / \     \
            3   4     8
               / \   /
              5   6 9

Boundary is:

1 → 2 → 3 → 5 → 6 → 9 → 8 → 7

Notice that 3, 5, 6, 9 are leaves, and the leaf nodes must be collected separately.

The Core Pattern

Think of the problem as three independent pieces:

             ROOT
               ↓
        ┌──────┴──────┐
        ↓             ↓
 LEFT BOUNDARY    RIGHT BOUNDARY
        ↓             ↓
        └─── LEAVES ──┘

More precisely:

Answer =
Root
+ Left Boundary
+ Leaves
+ Reverse(Right Boundary)
Important rules

For the left boundary:

if left child exists → go left
otherwise → go right

For the right boundary:

if right child exists → go right
otherwise → go left

And never add leaf nodes in the left/right boundary because they are already added by the leaf traversal.

Approach 1 — Brute Force: Store Parent + Leaf Information

A more brute-force way is to explicitly build:

parent relationship for every node
list of all leaves
path from root to the leftmost boundary leaf
path from root to the rightmost boundary leaf

This uses additional memory, but it helps understand what the boundary actually consists of.

The idea is:

Find all leaves
       ↓
leftmost leaf  → left boundary path
rightmost leaf → right boundary path
       ↓
all leaves
       ↓
combine

The leftmost leaf reached by always preferring left and otherwise right determines the left boundary path. Similarly, the rightmost leaf determines the right boundary path.

Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public ArrayList<Integer> boundaryTraversal(Node root) {

        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return ans;
        }

        if (isLeaf(root)) {
            ans.add(root.data);
            return ans;
        }

        Map<Node, Node> parent = new HashMap<>();
        ArrayList<Node> leaves = new ArrayList<>();

        buildParentAndLeaves(root, null, parent, leaves);

        // Left boundary = path root -> leftmost leaf, excluding leaf
        ArrayList<Node> leftPath = getPath(root, leaves.get(0), parent);

        for (int i = 0; i < leftPath.size() - 1; i++) {
            ans.add(leftPath.get(i).data);
        }

        // All leaves from left to right
        for (Node leaf : leaves) {
            ans.add(leaf.data);
        }

        // Right boundary
        Node rightmostLeaf = leaves.get(leaves.size() - 1);

        ArrayList<Node> rightPath =
                getPath(root, rightmostLeaf, parent);

        // If leftmost and rightmost leaf are same,
        // there is no separate right boundary.
        if (leaves.get(0) != rightmostLeaf) {
            for (int i = rightPath.size() - 2; i >= 1; i--) {
                ans.add(rightPath.get(i).data);
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }

    boolean isLeaf(Node root) {
        return root.left == null && root.right == null;
    }

    void buildParentAndLeaves(
            Node root,
            Node par,
            Map<Node, Node> parent,
            ArrayList<Node> leaves) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        parent.put(root, par);

        if (isLeaf(root)) {
            leaves.add(root);
            return;
        }

        buildParentAndLeaves(root.left, root, parent, leaves);
        buildParentAndLeaves(root.right, root, parent, leaves);
    }

    ArrayList<Node> getPath(
            Node root,
            Node target,
            Map<Node, Node> parent) {

        ArrayList<Node> path = new ArrayList<>();

        Node curr = target;

        while (curr != null) {
            path.add(curr);
            curr = parent.get(curr);
        }

        Collections.reverse(path);

        return path;
    }
}

