Approach 2 — Recursive Three-Part Traversal

This is the standard approach.

We break the problem into exactly three operations:

1. Add left boundary
2. Add all leaves
3. Add right boundary in reverse

The root is handled separately.

Why recursion is useful

For leaves, we need to visit the entire tree:

             1
           /   \
          2     7
         / \     \
        3   4     8
           / \   /
          5   6 9

A DFS naturally visits leaves from:

left → right
Step 1 — Add Left Boundary

Start from:

Node curr = root.left;

At every step:

if left exists:
    go left
else:
    go right

Example:

1 → 2 → 4
       ↓
       left exists
       ↓
       5

But only add non-leaf nodes.

Step 2 — Add Leaves

Do a DFS.

Whenever:

root.left == null && root.right == null

add the node.

Step 3 — Add Right Boundary

Start:

Node curr = root.right;

Go:

right if possible
otherwise left

Store the nodes temporarily.

At the end reverse them because the required traversal is:

bottom → top
