Why Leaves Must Be Excluded From Left/Right Boundary

This is one of the most common mistakes.

Suppose:

       1
      /
     2
    /
   3

3 is both:

left boundary

and:

leaf

If we add it in both traversals:

1,2,3,3

which is wrong.

Therefore:

Left Boundary → exclude leaves
Leaves         → include all leaves
Right Boundary → exclude leaves
Why Right Boundary Is Reversed

Suppose:

        1
         \
          2
           \
            3
             \
              4

When traversing downward, we get:

2 → 3

But anti-clockwise boundary traversal must come back upward:

3 → 2

Therefore we store:

[2,3]

and reverse:

[3,2]

Final:

1,4,3,2
Approach 3 — Iterative Version

We can remove recursion from the leaf traversal by using an explicit stack.

The idea remains exactly the same:

Root
 ↓
Left boundary
 ↓
Leaves
 ↓
Right boundary reversed

The difference is that addLeaves() uses a stack.

Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    boolean isLeaf(Node root) {
        return root.left == null && root.right == null;
    }

    void addLeftBoundary(Node root, ArrayList<Integer> ans) {

        Node curr = root.left;

        while (curr != null) {

            if (!isLeaf(curr)) {
                ans.add(curr.data);
            }

            if (curr.left != null) {
                curr = curr.left;
            } else {
                curr = curr.right;
            }
        }
    }

    void addLeaves(Node root, ArrayList<Integer> ans) {

        Deque<Node> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(root);

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {

            Node curr = stack.pop();

            if (isLeaf(curr)) {
                ans.add(curr.data);
                continue;
            }

            // Push right first so left is processed first
            if (curr.right != null) {
                stack.push(curr.right);
            }

            if (curr.left != null) {
                stack.push(curr.left);
            }
        }
    }

    void addRightBoundary(Node root, ArrayList<Integer> ans) {

        Node curr = root.right;
        ArrayList<Integer> temp = new ArrayList<>();

        while (curr != null) {

            if (!isLeaf(curr)) {
                temp.add(curr.data);
            }

            if (curr.right != null) {
                curr = curr.right;
            } else {
                curr = curr.left;
            }
        }

        for (int i = temp.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            ans.add(temp.get(i));
        }
    }

    public ArrayList<Integer> boundaryTraversal(Node root) {

        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return ans;
        }

        if (isLeaf(root)) {
            ans.add(root.data);
            return ans;
        }

        ans.add(root.data);

        addLeftBoundary(root, ans);
        addLeaves(root, ans);
        addRightBoundary(root, ans);

        return ans;
    }
}

