The Most Important Part of addLeftBoundry()

Your code:

if(curr.left!=null){
    curr=curr.left;
} else {
    curr=curr.right;
}

This handles irregular trees.

Example:

        1
       /
      2
       \
        4
         \
          5

There is no left child under 2.

So we don't stop.

Instead:

2 → right → 4 → right → 5

Thus the boundary can continue downward.

This is a very common binary-tree boundary pattern:

Prefer one direction.
If unavailable, fall back to the other.

For left boundary:

LEFT → RIGHT

For right boundary:

RIGHT → LEFT
The Most Important Part of the Entire Problem

Don't think of Boundary Traversal as one complicated traversal.

Think:

              Boundary
                  |
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ↓          ↓          ↓
     Root     Left/Leaves   Right
                Boundary

Or even better:

Answer =
Root
+ Left Boundary
+ All Leaves
+ Reverse(Right Boundary)
Pattern Recognition

When you encounter this problem in an interview:

Step 1

Ask:

Is the root a leaf?

If yes:

return [root]
Step 2

Add root.

Step 3

Walk down the left side:

left → right fallback

excluding leaves.

Step 4

DFS through the entire tree and add:

all leaves left → right
Step 5

Walk down the right side:

right → left fallback

excluding leaves.

Step 6

Reverse the right boundary.

